1795034661555459	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ap	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nSippycup now assesses the SIP/RTP edge well, but the phone media server's application layer is outside every executable path. The owner has confirmed an admin HTTP surface and believes an API exists; IVR, voicemail, conference, recording, media-library, WebSocket, and tenant surfaces are still unknown. The target profile, scope intake, campaign planner, and cockpit describe only SIP signaling and RTP media. Admin/API/WebSocket coverage exists only as an offline web-security profile compiler and evidence oracle (lib/sippycup_web_security/), with no live adapter, no route/role fixture, and no owner intake path.\n\nThis goal makes the application layer a first-class, approval-bound assessment surface without inventing routes, methods, credentials, or target behaviour the owner has not supplied.\n\nOutcome\n-------\n- One engagement scope can describe SIP/RTP, admin HTTP/API, WebSocket signaling, media-store objects, and multiple test identities/roles.\n- Scope compile produces a reviewable application-surface plan and adapter skeleton from owner answers; unresolved details are explicitly pending, not guessed.\n- The existing offline web-security checks have a live, fixed-route runner with the same authorization, literal-target, ceiling, evidence, and privacy guarantees as calls and campaigns.\n- Media-store/recording tests (anonymous access, role boundary, cross-tenant IDOR, path traversal, SSRF, signed-URL expiry) are executable against an approved target.\n- A local loopback fixture lets all of this be built and gate-tested while the owner is unavailable.\n- Multi-identity/tenant and secret-DTMF models make voicemail, conference, role, and tenant-boundary tests possible without putting PINs or real user data in plans.\n\nNon-goals\n---------\n- No generic HTTP request primitive, arbitrary URL/path/method/body, or unrestricted scanner.\n- No target traffic without a separately reviewed approval, literal destination, role, route whitelist, and hard ceilings.\n- No invented API details. Unknown surfaces stay pending.\n- No real user media or credentials in fixtures, plans, logs, or reports.\n\nChildren\n--------\nApplication-surface intake; local practice admin/API/media-store fixture; live admin HTTP/API runner; media-store and recording authorization checks; multi-identity/tenant model; secret DTMF input; IVR/voicemail/conference feature playbooks.","kind":"goal","labels":["app","api","security","roadmap"],"size":"xl","title":"Assess the phone media server application layer"}	blake3:dFNVSRx_9v0sO586K6x8STmqpCLpKscB2N7nWDAWazg
1795034776249624	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-65l	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nscope.yaml and the owner question sheet model only signaling, media, one account, and call destinations (lib/sippycup_workbench/scope_questions.py). schemas/target-profile-v1.schema.json has no admin HTTP/API or WebSocket surface. The web-security profile is a separate JSON island with its own authorization, limits, and adapter. The owner has confirmed an admin HTTP surface and thinks an API exists; IVR, voicemail, conference, recording, WSS, media-library, and tenant surfaces are unknown.\n\nWithout an intake path, any live application plan would invent routes or depend on hand-edited JSON outside the engagement record.\n\nOutcome\n-------\n- Add an optional application section to the scope document and question sheet: admin base URL(s), literal connect address and port, TLS server name/CA, browser vs API surface, WSS URL and allowed origins, roles/identities, tenant labels, media-store object classes, and a request for the owner's route inventory packet.\n- Unknown answers are recorded as pending/unknown, never inferred. The UI says unknown rather than not-applicable until the owner answers.\n- scope check validates the section. scope compile produces an application profile plus an adapter skeleton with fixed route IDs and no invented paths, methods, headers, bodies, or messages.\n- The compiled artifact binds approval reference/window, literal destinations, TLS trust, role/credential references, ceilings, and surfaces, and validates with the existing web-security contract.\n- status and the cockpit show application-surface readiness and exactly which questions are missing.\n- Old scope files and target profiles remain valid (optional section only).\n\nAcceptance criteria\n-------------------\n- Tests cover pending, incomplete, complete, and inconsistent scopes; no network; no secret values; unknown fields rejected; old file compatibility; compiled app plan validates; no path/method/body appears unless the owner supplied it.\n- Docs updated: SCOPE-INTAKE, WEB-SECURITY, CLI, WORKBENCH, COCKPIT; add a pending example scope.\n- No target traffic from init, check, compile, status, or cockpit.\n\nGuardrails\n----------\nDo not infer an API style from the admin web UI. Do not accept a hostname as a destination. A DNS name is a TLS identity only; every packet still goes to a literal approved address.","kind":"task","labels":["api","app","scope","ux"],"parent":"bn-2ap","size":"l","title":"Add application surfaces and test identities to scope intake and compiled plans"}	blake3:FD0HK_PL5R_jdJ_TC9J9d4QTxJCRJRJfGxCw8g-U6hk
1795034776357356	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3u3	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nThe owner is unavailable, so the real API shape is unknown. The live admin runner and media-store checks need a deterministic local target that can prove authentication, roles, object scope, CSRF, IDOR, path traversal, SSRF, signed-URL, rate-limit, and WebSocket behavior offline.\n\nOutcome\n-------\nAdd an optional practice fixture (a new Containerfile.practice-app or an extension of the practice target) that runs only on loopback in --network=none:\n- Admin HTTPS API: login/session, viewer/operator/admin roles, fixed read/write routes, CSRF check, session expiry, rate limit, object scope, canary recording/voicemail objects, predictable and opaque IDs, signed-URL expiry, a path-traversal trap, a redirect trap, and a URL-fetch endpoint that can call only an approved local HTTP canary.\n- WSS signaling: origin allow/deny, authentication required, role-bound messages, size/rate caps, and state-machine checks.\n- Deterministic per-up credentials and object IDs in tmpfs; canary audio only; no real user data.\n- A documented fake adapter and golden observations so the live runner and evidence path can be developed before the owner supplies the real adapter.\n\nAcceptance criteria\n-------------------\n- A command such as practice-target app up refuses any non-loopback address and fails closed unless the container is network-none.\n- make app-practice-gate exercises an offline plan and evidence round trip; every check has a positive and a negative case.\n- No egress, no secrets in argv/logs, read-only root filesystem, no real media, deterministic output.\n- PRACTICE-TARGET and WEB-SECURITY document the fixture, its limits, and how to replace it with a target adapter.\n\nGuardrails\n----------\nThis is a shape fixture, not a claim about the owner's API. It must not be promoted into a target adapter or treated as target authorization.","kind":"task","labels":["practice","api","admin","testing"],"parent":"bn-2ap","size":"l","title":"Build a loopback media-server practice fixture for admin HTTP/API, WSS, and media-store tests","urgency":"urgent"}	blake3:WBLcW9nBbCX6_hoIo8BdcUL04Nsd7fcYGuUgQ_MeBZE
1795034776469481	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-upd	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nlib/sippycup_web_security currently compiles and evaluates only (contracts.py, evidence.py). No command sends an approved admin HTTP/API or WSS case, enforces live ceilings, captures evidence, or handles cancellation and cleanup. The existing offline checks have no live counterpart.\n\nOutcome\n-------\nAdd web-security run PLAN --adapter ... (or an app run alias) that:\n- Revalidates the plan digest, approval window, literal connect addresses, TLS names/CA, roles, origins, route IDs, and every ceiling immediately before use.\n- Sends only the fixed route/operation/case IDs in the adapter. No arbitrary URL, method, header, body, or WebSocket frame is exposed.\n- Uses external credential refs (env/fd/exec), never puts values in argv, logs, plans, or artifacts, refuses redirects and unapproved destinations, and pins TLS to the reviewed identity.\n- Enforces max cases, connections, HTTP requests, WebSocket messages, auth failures, bytes, duration, and request rate. Stops on authorization lapse, unexpected destination, ceiling, target instability, or operator cancellation.\n- Writes a private run directory with normalized observations, scoped capture when approved, result.json, evidence manifest, and privacy lint. SIGINT/SIGTERM clean up and preserve evidence.\n- Reuses the campaign/guided-run safety patterns rather than inventing a second execution lifecycle.\n\nAcceptance criteria\n-------------------\n- Invalid, expired, mismatched, or replayed plans send zero packets; every request is counted; a request that would exceed a ceiling is refused before it is sent.\n- The practice fixture gate proves allowed, denied, rate-limited, expired, foreign-origin, role-boundary, CSRF, and object-scope outcomes.\n- A redirect or foreign origin never receives a request. Cancellation stops within the stated deadline and leaves truthful evidence.\n- A secret token or cookie appears nowhere in the run directory.\n- Command registry, help, docs, schema, and cockpit integration; no live target adapter is shipped.\n\nDependencies\n------------\nNeeds A1 (compiled application scope) and A2 (loopback fixture) before the live gate can pass.","kind":"task","labels":["authorization","live","api","security"],"parent":"bn-2ap","size":"xl","title":"Implement an approval-bound live runner for the fixed admin HTTP/API adapter"}	blake3:DMEWoXTdTBRYzmXHrpR0oyueL7h4bCBiEmlyovddXLU
1795034776579602	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2bc	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nA phone media server's most sensitive objects are recordings, voicemails, prompts, and media-library files. The current web-security catalog has generic admin checks but no media object classes, no canary-content verification, and no media-specific traversal, SSRF, or IDOR model.\n\nOutcome\n-------\nAdd a fixed, owner-approved media-store case pack:\n- Anonymous read, viewer/operator/admin read, same-tenant vs cross-tenant, predictable vs opaque ID, path traversal in object name or URL, signed-URL expiry, Range/cache behavior, redirect to a foreign host, directory listing, and URL-fetch SSRF against an approved canary.\n- Seed one canary recording or voicemail through an approved call/upload. Retrieve it from each role/tenant and verify SHA-256 and duration with media analyze. Raw audio is retained only under an explicit evidence policy.\n- Object IDs come from an owner-supplied list or an approved bounded range. No brute-force enumeration and no real user content.\n- The report labels object classes and role/tenant relationships, never raw IDs or media. Findings cite status, content-type, length, and canary match.\n\nAcceptance criteria\n-------------------\n- The practice fixture gate covers every positive and negative case. Cross-tenant access is denied by default and flagged when allowed.\n- SSRF and redirect cases can reach only the approved canary; no external egress.\n- Privacy lint passes with retainPayload=false. Canary proof works with retainPayload=true under explicit approval.\n- Docs and schema updated; no generic path or URL input.\n\nDependencies\n------------\nNeeds A3 (live runner) and A5 (identity/tenant model).","kind":"task","labels":["api","media","security","idor"],"parent":"bn-2ap","size":"l","title":"Add media-store and recording authorization checks to the live app adapter"}	blake3:prGHLra7EfZzXUvnT0jsW8a1Ne3MKa08U-5o9rPZnZQ
1795034776688105	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1uj	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nTarget profiles and campaigns carry one account per target, and each case has one target. Cross-role, cross-tenant, spoofed-identity, conference/voicemail isolation, and recording IDOR tests cannot be expressed without hand-editing separate profiles. The owner has not confirmed tenants, but the model must be ready and fail closed.\n\nOutcome\n-------\n- Add scope identities: id, role, tenant label, credential ref, permitted surfaces, and an owner approval reference for each.\n- Add relationships: same-tenant, cross-tenant, admin-vs-viewer, caller-vs-callee. A case may reference two identities only when the relationship is approved.\n- Compile identities into application profiles and campaign/target profiles, and expose them to the SIP softphone and HTTP runner without putting values in plans.\n- Enforce per-identity attempt and lockout caps plus global ceilings. Reject a cross-tenant case unless both identities and the relationship are approved.\n- Reports use role/tenant labels, not account identifiers, and show which boundary was tested.\n\nAcceptance criteria\n-------------------\n- Offline validation tests cover missing, unapproved, and cross-tenant identities; no secret values; old single-account profiles remain valid.\n- A two-identity practice case proves same-tenant allowed vs cross-tenant denied. A SIP identity-spoof or registration case can use the same model.\n- No live traffic without approval; docs and schemas updated.\n\nGuardrails\n----------\nDo not silently reuse one account for two roles. Do not label an unknown tenancy as same-tenant.","kind":"task","labels":["auth","tenancy","scope","safety"],"parent":"bn-2ap","size":"l","title":"Add a multi-identity and tenant-boundary model for application and SIP tests"}	blake3:t3VNC4-Au2nfMNlIBgJOJCxh8duxo4xmFIJ0WmylXg4
1795034776797879	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1yw	{"description":"Problem\n-------\ncall.dtmf is a literal string frozen into the plan, and the docs correctly warn not to put a real PIN in a manifest. Voicemail and conference PINs therefore cannot be tested safely. The softphone sends only RFC 4733 and never falls back, which is good, but the secret path is missing.\n\nOutcome\n-------\n- Add call.dtmfSecretRef (name only) or an equivalent --dtmf-secret-ref that resolves through the existing secret providers at execution time.\n- Keep the plan, logs, events, result, journal, and evidence free of the digits. Record only method, count, timing, and a redacted status. Do not hash short PINs.\n- Support the existing pause grammar and 1-32 digit bounds. One secret DTMF string per call. No retries. Enforce owner-approved attempt and lockout limits.\n- Fail closed when the ref is missing, the provider errors, or the call is not approved. No network in plan or dry-run.\n- Update guided call, campaign softphone runner, docs, tests, and privacy lint.\n\nAcceptance criteria\n-------------------\n- A test secret never appears in any artifact. Wrong or missing ref blocks before a packet.\n- A practice voicemail/conference case proves digits were sent and counted without storage.\n- No fallback to SIP INFO or in-band. No lockout bypass.\n\nGuardrails\n----------\nA secret DTMF ref is not a credential ref unless the owner explicitly treats it as one. Both must remain separately revocable.","kind":"task","labels":["credentials","dtmf","security","privacy"],"parent":"bn-2ap","size":"m","title":"Add secret DTMF input for voicemail and conference PIN tests"}	blake3:K306keJ9Jqb16iVxoHTyr9uC_8Hf5e95YQvf2YX--T0
1795034776907489	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3f0	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nThe campaign matrix declares 12 factors (transport, addressFamily, codec, ptime, mediaProtection, dtmf, earlyMedia, holdReinvite, teardownInitiator, duration, nat, impairment), but the softphone runner actively exercises only transport, address family, negotiated codec, plain/SDES-SRTP, RFC 4733 DTMF, hold=disabled, caller teardown, duration, and impairment=none. factor_report in lib/sippycup_softphone/campaign_runner.py:347 honestly lists the rest as notExercised, yet a generated case can still report passed while ptime, earlyMedia, hold, callee teardown, NAT, impairment, DTLS-SRTP, SIP INFO, or in-band DTMF were never sent.\n\nThe campaign capture filter also misses RTP at an approved media address that differs from the signaling host (bn-24x), contradicting the runner's media-scope check. Equivalent paths currently enforce media scope differently: call, matrix, and checks honor owner-approved media networks; campaign does not capture them.\n\nOutcome\n-------\n- Split media is captured and counted wherever the runner admits media.\n- Adapters declare supported factors and actions. Generated cases validate against that capability.\n- An unexercised factor is unknown/incomplete, not a pass, unless the manifest explicitly allows it with a reason.\n- The softphone/campaign runner can actually exercise ptime, early media, callee teardown, SIP INFO/in-band DTMF, hold/re-INVITE, and DTLS-SRTP.\n- The guided matrix command exposes the same real axes and practice coverage.\n\nNon-goals\n---------\nNo new load or concurrency behavior. No credential guessing. No target traffic outside the existing approval and ceiling model.","kind":"goal","labels":["campaign","matrix","coverage","quality","safety"],"size":"xl","title":"Make campaign and matrix coverage executable and truthful"}	blake3:CcphuGM8pw7-5rr1xb532JjgRQSU6ykPiVWYIgUNJRA
1795034777018180	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-6u3	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nMATRIX_FACTORS in lib/sippycup/campaign.py accepts 12 dimensions, but the selected runner cannot perform many of them. Compilation can create cases the adapter cannot exercise, and factor_report only annotates them. A matrix can look complete while large parts were never observed.\n\nOutcome\n-------\n- Define a versioned adapter capability document listing supported factors, supported values, supported actions, and evidence requirements.\n- Campaign planning validates every generated case and action against the selected adapter. Unsupported combinations are refused or marked incomplete at plan time, never silently generated.\n- Treat notExercised as unknown/incomplete per case. Add an explicit allowUnexercised manifest field with a reason, or an owner-approved requireExercised list. The default is fail closed.\n- The campaign matrix report and owner report show exercised vs not-exercised per cell and cannot call a cell pass when a declared factor was unobserved.\n- Keep old frozen plans valid: capability data is optional, and absent capability keeps the old annotated behavior with a documented limitation.\n\nAcceptance criteria\n-------------------\n- A simulated adapter that supports only transport and codec cannot compile a hold case. A hold case with allowUnexercised passes only when explicitly allowed. No false pass.\n- Practice gate has one case per supported factor; unsupported factors are visibly incomplete.\n- Docs and schema updated; no target traffic during planning.","kind":"task","labels":["campaign","coverage","quality","schema"],"parent":"bn-3f0","size":"l","title":"Add a campaign adapter capability contract and fail-closed factor coverage"}	blake3:49whjTKuTSj4k385KNK5nfGFRYexYtei-DQKU3uMwg0
1795034777126277	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3bn	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nThe campaign matrix declares ptime, earlyMedia, teardownInitiator, and dtmf=sip-info/inband, but the runner sets no ptime, does not assert early media, always hangs up as the caller, and only sends RFC 4733. The practice target already has earlymedia, noanswer, late, and drop destinations.\n\nOutcome\n-------\n- Add SDP ptime control (10/20/30/40/60) with validation and wire evidence.\n- Observe and assert 183/early media and RTP before answer. Distinguish missing, unexpected, and accepted early media.\n- Record and accept callee BYE and timeout cases. Do not classify them as caller teardown.\n- Send SIP INFO and in-band DTMF only when the case selects that method. Keep RFC 4733 as the default and never silently fall back.\n- Update factor_report/evaluate, result facts, docs, and practice cases.\n\nAcceptance criteria\n-------------------\n- The practice gate covers earlymedia, drop, noanswer, late, and dtmf destinations and reports the expected exercised/skipped facts.\n- A case declaring ptime, SIP INFO, or in-band cannot pass unless the method was actually used and observed.\n- No new retry or credential attempt; budgets unchanged.\n\nGuardrails\n----------\nDo not send in-band DTMF over an encrypted media path unless the codec path can actually carry it and the plan says so.","kind":"task","labels":["campaign","media","sip","coverage"],"parent":"bn-3f0","size":"l","title":"Add ptime, early media, callee teardown, and DTMF-method control to the softphone/campaign runner"}	blake3:fK4XiwB9caamaqjyb5X0Waz1Mp8Si8ZFYpMeqgN9FFU
1795034777232507	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-213	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nholdReinvite is a declared matrix factor but the runner never sends hold or re-INVITE. mediaProtection=dtls-srtp is declared but the softphone keys SDES only (--srtp-keying=0). A media server's hold/MOH and DTLS-SRTP behavior is therefore untested.\n\nOutcome\n-------\n- Add bounded hold/re-INVITE sendonly and inactive transitions, resume, and media observation. Use PJSUA console control or a reviewed driver path, not raw SIP injection.\n- Add DTLS-SRTP negotiation (keying/fingerprint) with certificate/fingerprint evidence. Never fall back to SDES or plain RTP. Report the negotiated profile.\n- Update factor_report/evaluate, result facts, schemas, docs, and the practice target with hold/MOH and DTLS-SRTP destinations.\n- Preserve one call per step, existing ceilings, and no retry on rejected credentials.\n\nAcceptance criteria\n-------------------\n- Practice gate proves hold/resume and DTLS-SRTP active/inactive outcomes. A DTLS case cannot pass with SDES.\n- Media stops and restarts at the expected transition. Evidence names the observed profile.\n- No secret key material in artifacts.\n\nDependencies\n------------\nB2 should land first so the capability contract can mark these factors supported.","kind":"task","labels":["campaign","media","webrtc","coverage"],"parent":"bn-3f0","size":"l","title":"Add hold/re-INVITE and DTLS-SRTP control to the softphone/campaign runner"}	blake3:ADe1I_jkxv20ZTYIKHezG9avwidQWN7DjpPDAoVPBeo
1795034777341112	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-ovc	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nSippycup observes posture but rarely demonstrates impact. There is no first-class listener that can receive a call, RTP, or HTTP request caused by the target. The tiny campaign-loopback-uas is a test fixture, not an operator tool. Without a collaborator, REFER/toll fraud, registration takeover, SDP redirect/RTP reflection, SSRF, webhook abuse, and cross-call leakage can only be inferred from target logs or packet captures.\n\nOutcome\n-------\n- A bounded sippycup canary service with SIP UAS, RTP listener, and HTTP/HTTPS callback, tokenized per test, privacy-safe, loopback or an approved test address only.\n- Canary-driven checks for registration takeover, REFER/transfer, identity spoofing, SDP redirect/RTP reflection, SSRF, and cross-call/tenant leakage.\n- PCAP-to-RTP extraction and DTMF detection feed media analyze and prove canary content.\n- Existing live torture (bn-ww5) and WebRTC live (bn-3vu) reuse the same collaborator and evidence patterns.\n\nNon-goals\n---------\nNo reflection or amplification. No unapproved destination. No raw audio or secret retention by default.","kind":"goal","labels":["canary","media","impact","security"],"size":"xl","title":"Prove impact with an approved canary collaborator and live media-plane tests"}	blake3:Ws0-ab_vufbLpAKOjhoueyODNDEP7ktIj93wSQkRzFg
1795034777446047	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-351	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nThere is no operator-grade listener to receive a target-initiated call, media stream, or HTTP request. The loopback UAS answers only a fixed test flow and does not log enough normalized evidence for a finding.\n\nOutcome\n-------\nAdd a bounded command, for example sippycup canary listen, with:\n- SIP UAS: log method, request URI class, selected headers, Call-ID hash, source tuple, and optional answer with reviewed SDP; support INVITE, BYE, CANCEL, REFER, MESSAGE, NOTIFY, and OPTIONS.\n- RTP listener: log source tuple, SSRC, payload type, packet count, timestamps, and payload hashes; optionally verify a deterministic canary.\n- HTTP/HTTPS callback: log method, path class, header names, body hash, and source; respond with a fixed reviewed status.\n- A per-test token embedded in the URI/path/Contact/Refer-To so the event can be correlated without storing secrets.\n- Strict event, packet, byte, duration, and concurrency caps; loopback or an approved literal test address; no outbound traffic except protocol responses.\n- JSONL events plus an evidence manifest; mode 0600; privacy-safe by default.\n\nAcceptance criteria\n-------------------\n- A local gate proves exact event counts, token correlation, caps, and clean shutdown.\n- No egress beyond responses to the observed peer; no target traffic until an approved plan starts it.\n- A finding can cite canary event IDs and hashes without exposing payload or identity.\n- Command registry, help, docs, schema, and evidence privacy tests.\n\nGuardrails\n----------\nThe canary is a receiver, not a scanner. It must not initiate SIP, RTP, or HTTP to a target.","kind":"task","labels":["canary","sip","rtp","http"],"parent":"bn-ovc","size":"xl","title":"Build the approved canary collaborator for SIP, RTP, and HTTP callbacks"}	blake3:pubxSLhZv_OI4jKkLTtmif5-z3I324OVxI4jYh2T-yc
1795034777555386	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3qq	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nThe checks catalog inventories REFER/MESSAGE/SUBSCRIBE but never tests whether they work unauthenticated. It never tests whether an authenticated REGISTER can replace a binding or whether the server trusts caller-supplied identity headers. These are high-impact call-control bugs on a media server and cannot be proven without a receiver.\n\nOutcome\n-------\nAdd fixed, owner-approved checks with exact request/action bounds:\n- Registration binding takeover: authenticated REGISTER with a canary Contact, then a call to the account, then a cleanup unregister. Prove whether the canary receives the INVITE.\n- REFER/transfer abuse: REFER with Refer-To pointing at the canary, then observe whether the server dials it; clean up the dialog.\n- Identity spoofing: INVITE with From/P-Asserted-Identity/Remote-Party-ID naming another approved test identity; observe whether the server accepts, rewrites, or rejects it.\n- Method authorization: one unauthenticated request for each advertised risky method, with correct dialog cleanup.\n- Findings: registration-binding-takeover, refer-toll-fraud, identity-spoof-accepted, method-works-unauthenticated.\n- One attempt, no retries, lockout-aware, stop on canary event or instability.\n\nAcceptance criteria\n-------------------\n- Practice target gate proves positive and negative cases and exact request counts.\n- No real user account is touched; only owner-approved test identities.\n- A finding includes canary event evidence and the matching capture frames.\n- Cleanup always removes any test binding and ends any dialog.\n\nDependencies\n------------\nNeeds C1 (canary) and A5 (multi-identity model).","kind":"task","labels":["auth","sip","canary","impact"],"parent":"bn-ovc","size":"l","title":"Add registration takeover, REFER/transfer, and identity-spoof checks using the canary"}	blake3:ppj2d9T_s4wr710tPsMzTl64XlSP6IMmSvT53VE5Klg
1795034777670918	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3i0	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nA media server sends RTP to the address in SDP. An attacker-controlled SDP can point that media at a third party, creating reflection or an internal SSRF path. Cross-call and cross-tenant media leakage is also unproven because the resilience oracles are synthetic.\n\nOutcome\n-------\n- Add a bounded SDP redirect/RTP-reflection case: offer c=/m= pointing at an approved canary RTP listener, place one call, and observe whether the target sends media there. Record source tuple, packet count, SSRC, and payload hash. No amplification and no retry.\n- Add a two-call cross-call isolation case with unique canary markers/SSRCs and two approved identities/tenants. Verify no marker, SSRC, tuple, or media appears in the other call.\n- Optionally cover media after teardown and tuple migration through the same canary.\n- Findings: sdp-media-reflection, cross-call-media-leak, cross-tenant-media-leak.\n- Stop on canary caps, unexpected source, or target instability.\n\nAcceptance criteria\n-------------------\n- Practice gate proves reflection to the canary and a clean no-leak baseline.\n- Two-identity/tenant cases are refused unless A5 relationships are approved.\n- Evidence uses canary hashes and event IDs, never raw audio by default.\n- No packet is sent outside the approved canary and target.\n\nDependencies\n------------\nNeeds C1 (canary) and A5 (identity/tenant model). Relates to bn-ww5 and the resilience oracles.","kind":"task","labels":["rtp","media","canary","isolation"],"parent":"bn-ovc","size":"l","title":"Add SDP redirect/RTP-reflection and cross-call isolation canary tests"}	blake3:IuZV9tV1BHiXlNo1R_f52dPutTKZFbrBSVfPYZO_ATY
1795034777782590	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3nn	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nmedia analyze consumes a raw payload file, but nothing in the tool extracts that payload from a capture. An operator must use tshark by hand, which breaks the reproducible evidence chain. There is also no standalone DTMF event summary outside the oracle.\n\nOutcome\n-------\n- Add media extract CAPTURE --codec ... --direction ... [--ssrc ...] [--out PATH] that uses the oracle's RTP correlation, bounds input size and packet count, and writes a payload or a hash/summary.\n- Add media dtmf CAPTURE to list RFC 4733 events, direction, timestamps, and source tuple without storing raw audio.\n- Default to privacy-safe output (hash, counts, duration); raw payload requires explicit retention.\n- Feed media analyze and the canary checks; include evidence references and a manifest entry.\n\nAcceptance criteria\n-------------------\n- A checked-in canary capture extracts byte-identically and media analyze reports the expected markers.\n- DTMF detection matches the oracle and the known practice sequences.\n- No raw audio by default; no unbounded pcap parse; exit codes distinguish malformed, encrypted, and unsupported.\n- Docs and tests.\n\nGuardrails\n----------\nThis is offline analysis. It must not re-parse arbitrary captures beyond the documented limits or write decoded audio without explicit retention.","kind":"task","labels":["evidence","media","privacy"],"parent":"bn-ovc","size":"m","title":"Add PCAP-to-RTP extraction and DTMF detection for impact evidence"}	blake3:C-FMY3Q_v3fLnN82A9iP8rlAvqRfX8g2fmGO6xpoS9M
1795034777886995	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-328	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nCONFIRMED by code inspection and a --dry-run through ./bin/sippycup: media send accepts a session whose remote SDP names 8.8.8.8:5353 and prints a packet plan for it. lib/sippycup_media/rtp.py:753 validates literal unicast and port >=1024 only; lib/sippycup_media/cli.py has no profile, approval, media-network, or media-port check. The live path would send the canary to that destination.\n\nThis violates the scope invariant that a first-class sender is bound to an approved engagement. The bounded 200-packet/1-second cap limits volume, not destination.\n\nOutcome\n-------\n- Add media send --profile PROFILE and validate the remote SDP against profile_scope() in lib/sippycup_workbench/media_scope.py: approved literal addresses, media networks, media port range, and the current approval window.\n- Require a profile for any non-loopback live send. A loopback-only unbound mode may remain for fixture work, clearly labelled.\n- Print the scope source and approval id in human and JSON output. Reject before opening a socket when the destination is outside scope.\n- Add tests for an out-of-scope address, out-of-range port, expired approval, and in-scope success.\n- Update MEDIA-SEND, CLI, and the command registry.\n\nAcceptance criteria\n-------------------\n- The out-of-scope fixture exits 2 with zero packets and no run artifact.\n- The in-scope fixture still sends and produces the same packet plan.\n- Dry-run remains network-free and shows the scope decision.\n- No secret or full SDP is copied into logs.\n\nRelates to bn-2jg (scope-safety audit).","kind":"bug","labels":["safety","scope","media"],"size":"m","title":"`media send` accepts a destination outside every approved profile and media network","urgency":"urgent"}	blake3:PHzws-DAq5LQsCuKaEbfbpLIEkV5mAUf3TXwUP2_Sko
1795034777998348	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3df	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nThe high-level call, checks, matrix, and campaign paths enforce a profile or manifest. Standalone preflight, media send, media-echo, shell, and the -- advanced escape hatch can send to operator-supplied destinations without an approval record. Some of that is intentional, but the output does not distinguish an approved sender from an unbound one, so an operator or agent cannot tell whether a command is inside the engagement.\n\nOutcome\n-------\n- Classify every first-class network command as scope-bound, optionally scope-bound, or unbound.\n- Add optional --profile to preflight and media send (media send is required by the D1 bug fix). Add --require-profile for CI or high-assurance use.\n- Print a consistent Scope line in human and JSON output: profile name, approval id/window, or UNBOUND with the operator-supplied target.\n- Make the cockpit and global help show the scope source before a LIVE action.\n- Keep the -- escape hatch explicitly UNBOUND; do not pretend to enforce it.\n\nAcceptance criteria\n-------------------\n- Output tests for approved, unbound, expired, and --require-profile cases.\n- An unbound command still runs when the operator explicitly accepts it; a scope-bound command fails closed without approval.\n- Docs and command registry updated; no change to existing profile-based paths.\n- Relates to bn-2jg and D1.\n\nGuardrails\n----------\nDo not add a generic --scope flag that re-implements profile validation. Reuse the existing profile/rehearse/media_scope code.","kind":"task","labels":["safety","scope","ux"],"size":"m","title":"Label every standalone sender as scope-bound or unbound and add optional profile binding"}	blake3:S5OK-T1gKwTbxQDrzTkMXUp1fVjdjtK0mgyI7xsXMzU
