1795034823911943	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2m5	{"description":"Problem\n-------\n./bin/sippycup matrix only grids transport x codec x SRTP, even though it is the one matrix path that already runs real guided calls, honors owner-approved media networks, and writes per-cell evidence. The campaign matrix declares ptime, earlyMedia, holdReinvite, teardownInitiator, and DTMF methods, but those axes have no real guided-call equivalent. Extending the campaign ledger without extending the real command would repeat the “declared but not exercised” problem.\n\nOutcome\n-------\n- Add reviewed axes such as --ptime, --early-media, --hold, --teardown, and --dtmf-method (or one closed --call-profile value per combination) to matrix plan/run.\n- Validate every axis against the profile's approvals and hard cell limit before any call. Refuse unsupported or unapproved values with exit 2.\n- Each cell uses the guided call path and records the observed factor facts: ptime on the wire, 183/RTP before answer, hold/re-INVITE and resume, who sent BYE, and the DTMF method actually used.\n- Distinguish not-run, not-exercised, pass, and fail in matrix.json and the owner report grid. Keep matrix.json v1 readable; new fields are optional and versioned.\n- Practice target up renders ready profiles for the supported axes; practice-target info prints the exact commands. Add a practice gate with one passing and one expected-failure case per axis.\n\nAcceptance criteria\n-------------------\n- plan and run --dry-run send nothing; an unapproved cell is refused before any packet.\n- A cell cannot pass if the declared axis was not exercised and observed.\n- Existing matrix.json v1 readers still work; new fields are optional.\n- Docs: CALL-MATRIX, PRACTICE-TARGET, ENGAGEMENT-REPORT, GUIDED-CALL.\n- No change to profile authorization or call ceilings beyond the existing limits.\n\nDependencies\n------------\nNeeds B3 (ptime/early media/teardown/DTMF method) and B4 (hold/re-INVITE). B2's capability contract should list the same axes.","kind":"task","labels":["matrix","cli","coverage","practice"],"parent":"bn-3f0","size":"l","title":"Extend the guided matrix command with call-control axes and practice coverage"}	blake3:OnPnX9qMAmKM6bID1hiYMkaGTX2qRV-UZ-4Z6YmecdU
1795034827916084	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-2m5	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-3bn"}	blake3:hqmrnbrUPCQv1TWGGDgLIRJlptO3S1ApTCqQS3kcSxI
1795034827935257	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-2m5	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-213"}	blake3:-t2E4hw4kj7XwswUe7pKH_QKmznjKWUebZ72d7tPE_s
1795034865602933	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-307	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nThe live admin runner needs fixed route IDs, operations, roles, and cases, but the owner may not know the API shape and the admin web UI cannot be explored without a separate approval. Manually reading an OpenAPI file, a browser HAR, or a JavaScript bundle is error-prone and easy to get wrong, and copying tokens or bodies into a plan would leak secrets.\n\nOutcome\n-------\nAdd an offline importer, for example web-security import-routes, that accepts one owner-supplied artifact beneath work/:\n- OpenAPI 3 or Swagger 2 JSON/YAML.\n- A browser HAR captured by the owner.\n- A JavaScript bundle from the admin console.\n\nIt extracts candidate method/path/operation IDs, parameters, request/response classes, auth hints, and content types; groups them into candidate route IDs; redacts Authorization, Cookie, Set-Cookie, tokens, query secrets, and request bodies; and writes a review document. It never sends traffic, never executes JavaScript, never follows a URL, and never approves a route. The reviewer selects the candidate IDs and writes the adapter; the existing compiler then validates it.\n\nAcceptance criteria\n-------------------\n- No network, no DNS, no JavaScript execution; input size and entity counts bounded; path traversal and symlinks rejected.\n- Deterministic output for the same artifact; no secret values or raw bodies; unknown formats fail closed with a clear message.\n- Candidate routes are explicitly unreviewed and cannot be promoted to an approved plan without human selection.\n- Fixtures for OpenAPI, HAR, and a small JS bundle; docs and tests.\n- A missing or malformed artifact sends nothing and writes no run directory.\n\nGuardrails\n----------\nThis is an offline aid, not a scanner. A route appearing in a bundle or HAR is an observation, not authorization or proof that the route exists on the target.","kind":"task","labels":["api","app","offline","adapter"],"parent":"bn-2ap","size":"l","title":"Add an offline admin API route extractor for owner-supplied OpenAPI, HAR, and JS bundle artifacts"}	blake3:VaPCtaNYQHWv_N8DEzW8Q6HyGVTCo7WhyR8Q8pYD3uc
1795034871296523	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1q4l	{"description":"Problem\n-------\nThe web-security catalog focuses on auth, role boundary, CSRF, session expiry, object scope, and rate limit. It has no baseline browser-security checks for the confirmed admin HTTP surface: security headers, HSTS, cookie flags, CORS policy, allowed methods, cache-control on sensitive responses, error-page disclosure, HTTP-to-HTTPS redirect, or version banners. These are cheap, high-signal findings and do not need the full route inventory.\n\nOutcome\n-------\nAdd a fixed baseline case pack to the live admin runner:\n- Response headers: HSTS, CSP, X-Content-Type-Options, X-Frame-Options/frame-ancestors, Referrer-Policy, Permissions-Policy; report missing/weak, no false “missing” on a redirect that never reaches the app.\n- Cookies: Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite, Domain/Path scope, expiry on session cookies; never log the value.\n- CORS: approved origin, foreign origin, null origin, preflight allowed methods/headers/credentials; one request per case.\n- Methods: OPTIONS/TRACE/HEAD and one unapproved method against an approved read route; correct Allow and no state change.\n- Sensitive caching: Cache-Control/Pragma/Expires on a session or object response; no body retained.\n- Error handling: one approved malformed request and one missing object; no stack trace, version, path, token, or internal host.\n- Redirects: HTTP-to-HTTPS, trailing-slash, and login redirect; no redirect to a foreign host.\n- Findings: security-header-missing, cookie-flag-missing, cors-wildcard/credentials, trace-enabled, sensitive-response-cacheable, error-disclosure, unsafe-redirect.\n- Fixed, owner-approved paths only; no directory brute force.\n\nAcceptance criteria\n-------------------\n- Practice fixture gate covers each positive and negative case.\n- Each case declares and enforces its request bound; no redirect is followed outside the approved origin.\n- No cookie/token/body value appears in output; privacy lint passes.\n- Findings are evidence-backed and severity-ranked; docs and schema updated.\n\nDependencies\n------------\nNeeds A3 (live runner). Paths and origins come from A1's application scope.","kind":"task","labels":["api","admin","http","security"],"parent":"bn-2ap","size":"m","title":"Add admin HTTP baseline checks for headers, cookies, CORS, methods, and error pages"}	blake3:y0OaE27_wEZcZ7dEqAgyEbvvuNbywVz1pW2rRb-bWN4
1795034875454648	bob-dev	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-1q4l	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-upd"}	blake3:I9B4JGLFgDJhBHp1fGRAvrsdIKyO8KGXxRJ7F-aLGAo
